// PAIR 6 of 6, root nllmoves.B — A PAIR IN WHICH NEITHER HALF CLOSES ANYTHING,
// and the reader's whole population is MADE by the producer:
//  · `each_pat_binding_place`'s PC::Struct arm handed a SHORTHAND field
//    `TypeRef(nullptr)`, so every consumer that gates on the binding type — the
//    by-value sub-place move rule among them — skipped `match x { Foo { f } =>
//    … }` exactly as it skips an array pattern. Traced one token apart on this
//    very program: `Foo::F1(p)` recorded `place=x.0`, `Foo { f }` recorded
//    NOTHING. A missing line has no spelling, which is why no grep-defined
//    class contained it. Measured alone: 72 arrivals, 0 rows, 0 legal programs
//    — it produces a record nobody reads.
//  · `take_borrow_whole_`'s third line is `if (it->moved)` and it never asked
//    `moved_fields`, the asymmetry `recvpartial` closed at the method-call
//    receiver one route over. Measured alone: ZERO matches — not an unreached
//    site, but an EMPTY population, because until a shorthand field got a type
//    nothing put a struct pattern's field into `moved_fields` at all.
// 0 + 0 = 0 and the whole is 1. Blame is per site, CREDIT IS PER SET.
// ONE TOKEN from pass/bc_partpair_struct_ref_field_twin: `Foo { f }` there is
// `Foo { ref f }`, which moves nothing and leaves `x` whole.
package bc_partpair_struct_shorthand_field_moved;

struct A { v: i64 }
impl Drop for A { fn drop(self: &mut A) {} }
struct Foo { f: A }

fn touch(a: &Foo) -> i64 { return a.f.v; }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let x = Foo { f: A { v: 1i64 } };
    match x {
        Foo { f } => {
            let _ = f.v;
        }
    }
    let _ = touch(&x);
    return 0i32;
}
